# Financial & State Invariants

## Ledger
The wallet is ledger-based. Ledger entries are authoritative and immutable in normal operation. Cached balances are projections only.

Required invariants:
- Every commission has earned_at, available_at, eligible base, amount and commission-rate/rule snapshot.
- Default commission hold is 15 days per qualifying payment/settlement policy.
- Held funds are not withdrawable.
- Only Available funds may be reserved for payout.
- Payout reservation is atomic and race-safe.
- Refund/chargeback creates reversal/adjustment records; never rewrite historical ledger entries.
- If a paid commission is later reversed, preserve the Paid payout and create the corrective negative entry/receivable according to policy.
- Manual credit/debit requires amount, direction, reason, actor and timestamp.
- Financial reports derive from authoritative payment/ledger data.

## Payment flow
Invoice -> processor attempt -> payment settlement -> exactly-once commission calculation -> hold -> entitlement activation/renewal -> notifications.
Retries must never double-settle or double-create commission.

## Commission snapshots
A later commission-rate change must never rewrite historical earnings. Effective-date rules apply only to qualifying transactions after their effective timestamp.

## Payout flow
Requested -> validated -> atomically reserved -> Approved/Rejected/Frozen -> Processing -> Paid. Mark Paid requires required private proof according to platform settings. Paid generates an agency-visible receipt.

## State machines
Centralize transitions and allowed-next-state rules for Agency, Customer, KYC, Agent, PhoneNumber, Invoice/Payment, Commission and Payout. Reject illegal transitions.

## Risk precedence
Risk/chargeback restrictions may override normal commercial state. A confirmed chargeback can freeze the customer and disable its agents according to the SRS risk policy. Commission reversal must follow the defined priority and preserve history.

## Concurrency
For money, reservation, inventory and idempotent webhook handling:
- use database transactions/appropriate isolation;
- lock or atomically update authoritative rows where needed;
- enforce unique idempotency/provider-event constraints;
- test concurrent requests explicitly;
- make retry behavior deterministic.
